From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Fri, 9 Dec 1994 13:07:06 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-376] SING: Post-singularity morals

(Note:  I think the "singularity" will take an interval of time,
not a point on the timeline.  I use the term to denote a set of effects.)

I think that the most important question about the Singularity is,
What could post-singularity morals look like?

Consider the present situation.  Our moral systems model the moral world
as consisting of interactions between intelligent individuals.  Most of us
think that only humans are moral agents, and only humans have rights.


PROBLEM 1: LEVELS OF INTELLIGENCE

This works only because there is a large gulf between human and other
animal intelligence.  Post-singularity, intelligence will exist on a
continuum.  We will have to confront the problem of moral relations
between creatures of different levels of intelligence.

Anarcho-capitalists tend towards Nietzschian beliefs
(the eternal struggle is good and strengthens everyone -- similar
to the way market forces create efficiency), or towards nihilism.
I think that if these memes are dominant post-Singularity, the Universe
will either (1.) be filled with unrelenting war between all manners of beings,
forever, or (2.) be the private preserve of a few gods.

The first option, "every man for himself", is preferable.  This solution has
worked well in evolution, and a true Nietzschian would say it was almost
a definition of the good.  But I wouldn't enjoy it.

In any case, I don't know if option (1) is possible.  Look at what happened 
n evolution.  The evolution of humans was a Singularity.  We now appropriate
somewhere around half of the Earth's land biomass.
(I've read 2 figures this week, one 40%, one 35% to 85%, but I
forgot where :( .  Suffice it to say we control the Earth.)
We are marginalizing all other species, preserving them only if they are
useful to us, exterminating or altering them if they are not.

(Consider a poodle: Five thousand years ago, its ancestors were wolves,
had a supportive social structure, and were far more intelligent.
I love dogs, and they are better companions for us than wolves, which
are too intelligent to consider their desires subservient to ours.
But the fact that the hand of man has touched the wolf and turned it
into a poodle holds a certain horror.)

What we do to others may soon be done to us.  If our ethics allow us
to treat animals any way we desire, then they would allow an Uberman
to treat us any way he desires.  There will be no war of evolution,
any more than there is war between humans and whales.  Just one-sided
exploitation.

The analogy of different post-Singularity species with biological niches
fails because of this.  There will be no room for less powerful beings
except as tools or pets of more powerful beings.  You will be either a
god, or a poodle.

Extropians, I suspect, don't fret over this too much because they hope
to be gods.  They assume that everyone will want to increase their
intelligence indefinitely, and so no one would willingly occupy the
lower levels anyway.  This may not be the case.  I know people who think
they would enjoy the lifestyle of a wolf more than that of a human.
Others may find themselves more comfortable as humans, or somewhere a
few rungs above us on the intellectual ladder.
They should have the freedom to choose that option,
and not be forced into a brains race to preserve their independence.

That reasoning motivated me to ask this list the following Y/N question:

>9.  Intellect is the greatest virtue, and creatures with greater intellect
>should eventually replace all creatures of lesser intellect.

By now you understand why I fear people who give a Yes answer to this
question.  I received 2 No's, 3 Yes's, and 3 wordy explanations that
I interpreted as taking a practical but not a moral position.

I want a system where each niche has autonomy.
The greatest diversity, the most complex interaction, the strangest and
most beautiful systems arise when competition is fair -- something
anarcho-capitalists should appreciate.  This requires the most powerful
beings to allocate some resources to less powerful beings, without
seizing control of their evolution.  The only reason I can think of
for doing so is a moral imperative.

I think such a moral imperative is compatible with Nietzschianism.
It is a variant in which, instead of desiring the production of one strong
and beautiful individual as king of the hill, you desire a strong and
beautiful universe.

I propose complexity as the fundamental value of such a Nietzschian variant.
Measuring complexity is, well, complex, but clearly a universe of
diverse creatures with diverse interactions would be more complex than a
monoculture.  Also, we get for free a dictum against violence:  destruction
increases entropy, which is the inverse of complexity.

So you see, I am not an environmentalist because watching sunsets gives me
warm squishy feelings.  It is the working out of a value system which I
think we need to preserve free, independent competition and interaction
through the Singularity.


PROBLEM 2: IDENTIFICATION OF INDIVIDUALS

Individuals possess rights.  But, post-singularity, drawing the lines
around individuals may be hard.  When intelligences join together
in networks, and those networks into larger networks, is each level
an individual, and does it have the right to suppress its constituent
individuals?  Can it allow its constituent individuals to violate each
others' rights, as we may suppress parts of our personality?
If you have five daemons in your mind, and you suppress one, is it
oppression?  If you join a collective personality, and it decides to
remake you, is it murder?  What if you have partial membership in
several larger individuals?  What if "you" share your memories with
others (possibly for a price), and "you" gain some of their memories --
if you go far enough in that direction, you will come to the point where
your mental content is not concentrated in any one information locus
identifiable as "you".  Etc.


Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu

